# WHERE EVERY PIECE OF OTHER PEOPLE'S WORK IN THIS TREE CAME FROM.
#
# The presence of this file is the declaration. It switches on the RichOS
# engine's guard-vendoring-commits.sh, which refuses a commit that adds files
# under a redistributable path with no entry here; and it is read by
# guard-dialect.sh, which leaves third-party bytes alone because of it. Delete
# it and both stand down — which is a visible, reviewable diff, and is meant
# to be.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS
# -------------
# On 2026-09-04 a publication audit asked a question nothing in this repository
# could answer: for each of the 27 skills under `engine/skills/`, did RichOS
# write it? Fifteen had not been written by RichOS. Exactly ONE of the fifteen
# had ever been written down as vendored, and it was written down in a COMMIT
# MESSAGE. Establishing the other fourteen took a full agent doing byte
# comparisons against upstream repositories, and two of them came back "high
# confidence" rather than certain, because by then the evidence had decayed.
#
#   THE FACT WAS KNOWN AT THE MOMENT OF VENDORING, BY THE PERSON DOING IT, AND
#   IT WAS NOT WRITTEN DOWN. EVERYTHING AFTER THAT WAS RECONSTRUCTION.
#
# The same missing fact caused a second failure, in a completely different
# mechanism. On 2026-08-30, commit 06f4a8221a61, this project's dialect sweep
# Americanized FOURTEEN lines across TWO vendored, MIT-licensed skills — four in
# `engine/skills/copywriting/references/natural-transitions.md` (Corey Haines's
# prose) and ten in `engine/skills/landing-page-taste/SKILL.md`. Neither is ours
# to edit. guard-dialect.sh was working exactly as designed; it simply had no way
# to know those files were somebody else's, because nothing in this repository
# knew.
#
# And the damage was a ONE-WAY DOOR, which is the sharper half. The same guard
# REFUSES a write carrying a non-American word, so re-vendoring either file
# verbatim from upstream was also blocked — and so was the attempt to QUOTE the
# two spellings into a notice explaining the difference, which was refused twice
# before somebody phrased the prose around it. The guard caused the divergence,
# forbade the repair, and forbade documenting it.
#
# So the fact stops being something a person remembers. It is data, here, in
# one place, and two mechanisms read it.
#
# WHY A REGISTRY AND NOT A DOCUMENT
# ---------------------------------
# `docs/legal/THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md` is the human-facing inventory and stays
# exactly that: prose, tables, the argument for each row. It is the right shape
# for a reader deciding what they may reuse, and the wrong shape for a guard —
# a guard that parsed a markdown table would be reading a document written for
# a person, and the first reformatting would silently turn enforcement off.
#
# A ledger a human is trusted to update becomes the next thing an audit
# reconstructs. So this one is not maintained by trust: a commit that adds
# material under a redistributable path with no entry here is REFUSED, and the
# refusal names the path and the line to add.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT DO
# --------------------------
# It never GUESSES provenance. It records what somebody wrote down. The guard
# asks one question and only one — "was this fact recorded?" — because a guard
# that infers origin is a guard that is confidently wrong some of the time, and
# a confidently wrong provenance claim is worse than an absent one.
#
# It is also not a license-compliance checker. Whether the bundled copy carries
# its upstream LICENSE file, and whether a modified copy says how it differs,
# are questions `docs/legal/THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md` answers and a human decides.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REDISTRIBUTABLE_PATHS — the paths this contract governs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Space-separated, repository-root-relative directory prefixes that hold
# material RichOS REDISTRIBUTES: bytes that leave this repository inside a
# published artifact and that could therefore be somebody else's work under
# somebody else's terms. A commit adding a file under one of these needs an
# entry below.
#
# DERIVED FROM WHERE THIRD-PARTY MATERIAL ACTUALLY LIVES, not from a guess at
# where it might. Every path named here holds third-party work today, evidenced
# in `docs/legal/THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md` and
# `docs/legal/SKILL-PROVENANCE-2026-09-04.md`:
#
#   engine/skills    15 of 27 skills are third-party (the whole 2026-09-04
#                    finding). This is the surface the defect happened on.
#   engine/tools     `gpt-exporter`, vendored from WebDevBooster/gpt-exporter.
#   app/ui/fonts     four font families under the SIL Open Font License,
#                    redistributed inside the application bundle.
#
# CONSIDERED AND DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED, because a guard that fires where no
# third-party material has ever lived is the cries-wolf failure that gets a
# guard switched off within a day:
#
#   app/crates, app/src-tauri   Rust dependencies arrive through Cargo and are
#                    inventoried by app/scripts/dependency-license-inventory.sh
#                    from the committed lockfiles. Nothing is vendored by hand.
#   app/ui/assets    brand material, governed by docs/legal/BRAND-ASSETS.md.
#   tools/           four RichOS-authored components. No third-party work has
#                    ever been vendored there. WIDEN THIS LIST the day that
#                    changes — it is one edit, in the file the vendoring
#                    happens next to.
#   engine/scripts, engine/reference, docs/   engine source and record. Not a
#                    redistribution surface for other people's work.
REDISTRIBUTABLE_PATHS="engine/skills engine/tools app/ui/fonts"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ENTRIES
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TAB-SEPARATED, one line per thing, ten columns, in this order:
#
#   1  path        repository-root-relative path this entry covers. A directory
#                  covers everything beneath it. Several paths for ONE thing
#                  (a font family is two files and a license) are separated by
#                  COMMAS — one line still describes one thing.
#   2  origin      `third-party` or `richos`. RichOS-authored material under a
#                  redistributable path is recorded too, and that is not
#                  bookkeeping: without it, writing a new skill of our own
#                  would be refused by a guard that cannot tell "not recorded"
#                  from "not ours". Recording authorship IS the fact.
#   3  license     SPDX identifier. RichOS-authored material is AGPL-3.0-only,
#                  the license of the repository around it.
#   4  holder      copyright holder, as the upstream states it.
#   5  upstream    where it came from. `-` for RichOS-authored.
#   6  revision    the pinned revision, or the version the copy declares.
#                  `unpinned` when neither was recorded at acquisition — an
#                  honest gap, not a dash that could be read as "n/a".
#   7  arrived     ISO date the bytes first landed in a RichOS repository, with
#                  the commit that did it. `history-floor:` marks a date that
#                  is only the oldest surviving commit rather than the real
#                  arrival — richos history was rewritten on 2026-08-29 and
#                  cannot answer for anything before 2026-08-20.
#   8  confidence  `certain`, `high`, or `medium-high`. NEVER rounded up. The
#                  provenance audit qualified two verdicts and both keep their
#                  qualification here; an entry that reads `certain` means a
#                  byte comparison against a named revision settled it.
#   9  modified    `verbatim`, or what differs from upstream. Apache-2.0 4(b)
#                  makes this an obligation for one row; everywhere else it is
#                  the thing that stops a future byte comparison looking like a
#                  mystery.
#  10  evidence    where the proof lives.
#
# WHY TAB-SEPARATED. One line is one entry, so a diff shows a whole fact
# changing rather than a key moving; `cut -f1` answers the guard's only
# question with no parser and no dependency; a missing field is an empty column
# a reader can SEE, where a missing key in a block format is invisible; and the
# engine already keeps data this way in scripts/lib/dialect-en-US.dict.
#
# Comment lines and blank lines are ignored. Fields must not contain tabs.

